1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,010
The voice you're hearing
right now... isn't me.

2
00:00:03,360 --> 00:00:04,050
Well.

3
00:00:04,110 --> 00:00:05,340
It is me.

4
00:00:05,340 --> 00:00:08,090
It's a clone of my voice, made with AI.

5
00:00:08,110 --> 00:00:10,530
And if I can do that with my own voice...

6
00:00:10,530 --> 00:00:12,660
someone else can do it with yours.

7
00:00:12,660 --> 00:00:17,240
That's one of five dangers hiding
inside the AI you use every day.

8
00:00:17,240 --> 00:00:18,510
The weather on your phone.

9
00:00:18,510 --> 00:00:20,510
The chatbot that settles an argument.

10
00:00:20,510 --> 00:00:22,620
The app that fixes your photos.

11
00:00:22,620 --> 00:00:24,240
Here's how I think about it.

12
00:00:24,240 --> 00:00:27,640
AI is like a brilliant stranger
who's moved into your phone.

13
00:00:27,640 --> 00:00:28,800
Super helpful.

14
00:00:28,800 --> 00:00:29,670
Never sleeps.

15
00:00:29,670 --> 00:00:31,390
Knows a bit about everything.

16
00:00:31,390 --> 00:00:33,910
But would you tell a
stranger your secrets?

17
00:00:33,910 --> 00:00:35,900
Let him decide who gets a job?

18
00:00:35,900 --> 00:00:38,160
Believe every photo he shows you?

19
00:00:38,160 --> 00:00:40,870
Trust him when he sounds
exactly like your mum?

20
00:00:40,870 --> 00:00:42,340
Let's go through all five.

21
00:00:42,340 --> 00:00:45,300
And stay to the end, because there's
one free trick that protects

22
00:00:45,300 --> 00:00:46,700
your whole family.

23
00:00:46,700 --> 00:00:48,120
Danger number one.

24
00:00:48,120 --> 00:00:49,380
Your secrets.

25
00:00:49,380 --> 00:00:51,580
People tell chatbots everything.

26
00:00:51,580 --> 00:00:52,470
Health worries.

27
00:00:52,470 --> 00:00:53,420
Money problems.

28
00:00:53,420 --> 00:00:54,490
Work documents.

29
00:00:54,490 --> 00:00:55,460
The lot.

30
00:00:55,460 --> 00:00:59,770
In 2023, an engineer at Samsung
pasted secret source code into

31
00:00:59,770 --> 00:01:02,810
ChatGPT, asking it to help fix a problem.

32
00:01:02,810 --> 00:01:05,870
Another shared a whole meeting
so it could write the minutes.

33
00:01:05,870 --> 00:01:07,150
Samsung's answer?

34
00:01:07,150 --> 00:01:10,570
It banned staff from using
tools like ChatGPT at work.

35
00:01:10,570 --> 00:01:11,930
For a while, anyway.

36
00:01:11,930 --> 00:01:14,570
And what happens to all that
depends on the company.

37
00:01:14,570 --> 00:01:16,930
Some use your chats to
train their next model.

38
00:01:16,930 --> 00:01:19,870
Some have staff who review
conversations for safety.

39
00:01:19,870 --> 00:01:23,080
And none of it is protected the
way a chat with your doctor is.

40
00:01:23,080 --> 00:01:24,970
Plus... things leak.

41
00:01:24,970 --> 00:01:29,510
In March 2023, a bug in ChatGPT
let some users see the titles

42
00:01:29,510 --> 00:01:31,260
of other people's chats.

43
00:01:31,260 --> 00:01:33,460
And for a small number
of paying subscribers,

44
00:01:33,460 --> 00:01:35,690
it may have shown their name, their email,

45
00:01:35,690 --> 00:01:37,820
and the last 4 digits of their card...

46
00:01:37,820 --> 00:01:39,090
to someone else.

47
00:01:39,090 --> 00:01:41,910
OpenAI took ChatGPT offline, fixed it,

48
00:01:41,910 --> 00:01:44,590
and said full card numbers
were never exposed.

49
00:01:44,590 --> 00:01:47,570
But it shows you just how
sensitive this stuff is.

50
00:01:47,570 --> 00:01:48,780
So here's the rule.

51
00:01:48,780 --> 00:01:52,560
Don't type anything into a chatbot
that you wouldn't write on a postcard.

52
00:01:52,560 --> 00:01:54,670
And switch off training in your settings.

53
00:01:54,670 --> 00:01:59,190
In ChatGPT, that's Data controls,
"Improve the model for everyone".

54
00:01:59,190 --> 00:02:00,620
Danger number two.

55
00:02:00,620 --> 00:02:01,740
Fairness.

56
00:02:01,740 --> 00:02:03,780
We like to think computers are neutral.

57
00:02:03,780 --> 00:02:05,410
But AI learns from us.

58
00:02:05,410 --> 00:02:06,510
From our history.

59
00:02:06,510 --> 00:02:08,740
And our history isn't always fair.

60
00:02:08,740 --> 00:02:10,020
Here's a famous one.

61
00:02:10,020 --> 00:02:14,130
Amazon once built an experimental AI
to sort through job applications.

62
00:02:14,130 --> 00:02:18,250
It learned from ten years of
CVs the company had received.

63
00:02:19,320 --> 00:02:21,950
And most of those CVs came from men.

64
00:02:22,190 --> 00:02:25,000
Tech has been a man's
world for a long time.

65
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:28,840
So the AI taught itself that
men were the better candidates.

66
00:02:28,880 --> 00:02:32,230
It marked down CVs with the
word "women's" in them.

67
00:02:32,230 --> 00:02:33,020
Like...

68
00:02:33,020 --> 00:02:35,250
"captain of the women's chess club".

69
00:02:35,250 --> 00:02:39,110
It even downgraded graduates
of two all-women's colleges.

70
00:02:39,110 --> 00:02:43,050
To be fair, Amazon's recruiters
never let it make the final call.

71
00:02:43,050 --> 00:02:45,800
And the company scrapped it,
because it couldn't guarantee the

72
00:02:45,800 --> 00:02:48,540
AI wouldn't find new ways to discriminate.

73
00:02:48,540 --> 00:02:50,490
But here's the scary part.

74
00:02:50,490 --> 00:02:53,300
When a person is unfair, you can see it.

75
00:02:53,360 --> 00:02:55,110
When a machine is unfair...

76
00:02:55,150 --> 00:02:56,690
it looks like maths.

77
00:02:56,690 --> 00:02:59,120
And that makes it so much harder to spot,

78
00:02:59,120 --> 00:03:00,430
and to challenge.

79
00:03:00,430 --> 00:03:03,320
And AI now helps make
decisions about jobs,

80
00:03:03,320 --> 00:03:05,450
loans, even healthcare.

81
00:03:05,450 --> 00:03:08,980
Here in the UK, if a really
important decision about you is made

82
00:03:08,980 --> 00:03:13,410
only by a machine, the law says you
can ask for a human to look at it.

83
00:03:13,410 --> 00:03:14,630
So ask.

84
00:03:14,630 --> 00:03:16,270
Danger number 3.

85
00:03:16,310 --> 00:03:17,500
Your eyes.

86
00:03:17,500 --> 00:03:19,780
Seeing used to be believing.

87
00:03:19,820 --> 00:03:20,900
Not anymore.

88
00:03:20,900 --> 00:03:25,960
In May 2023, a photo of an explosion
near the Pentagon went viral.

89
00:03:25,960 --> 00:03:27,110
Black smoke.

90
00:03:27,110 --> 00:03:29,570
Shared by accounts that looked official.

91
00:03:29,570 --> 00:03:30,620
It was fake.

92
00:03:30,620 --> 00:03:32,030
Made with AI.

93
00:03:32,030 --> 00:03:34,730
But US stocks still dipped
for a few minutes...

94
00:03:34,730 --> 00:03:38,090
before officials confirmed
there'd been no explosion at all.

95
00:03:38,090 --> 00:03:41,530
The same year, after President
Biden announced he'd run again,

96
00:03:41,530 --> 00:03:45,200
the Republican National Committee
answered with an ad built entirely

97
00:03:45,200 --> 00:03:46,760
from AI images.

98
00:03:46,760 --> 00:03:49,500
A made-up, dark future if he won.

99
00:03:49,500 --> 00:03:51,950
And the problem isn't one fake photo.

100
00:03:51,950 --> 00:03:55,460
It's what happens when anyone
can make "proof" of anything.

101
00:03:55,460 --> 00:03:57,540
If we can't agree on what's real...

102
00:03:57,540 --> 00:03:59,350
how do we agree on anything?

103
00:03:59,350 --> 00:04:03,680
So when something online makes you
angry or scared in two seconds flat...

104
00:04:03,680 --> 00:04:04,870
that's your cue.

105
00:04:04,870 --> 00:04:06,080
Slow down.

106
00:04:06,080 --> 00:04:07,640
Who posted it first?

107
00:04:07,640 --> 00:04:10,230
Is any real news outlet reporting it?

108
00:04:10,230 --> 00:04:12,030
Check it somewhere you trust.

109
00:04:12,030 --> 00:04:13,630
Danger number 4.

110
00:04:13,630 --> 00:04:15,880
And this is where it gets personal.

111
00:04:15,880 --> 00:04:17,730
Remember my voice at the start?

112
00:04:17,730 --> 00:04:20,950
Starling Bank says scammers can
clone a voice from as little as

113
00:04:20,950 --> 00:04:22,750
3 seconds of audio.

114
00:04:22,750 --> 00:04:23,970
A birthday video.

115
00:04:23,970 --> 00:04:25,000
A voice note.

116
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:26,850
A clip you posted online.

117
00:04:26,850 --> 00:04:28,280
Then the phone rings.

118
00:04:28,280 --> 00:04:29,520
And it's your daughter.

119
00:04:29,520 --> 00:04:30,560
Or your dad.

120
00:04:30,560 --> 00:04:31,460
Crying.

121
00:04:31,460 --> 00:04:32,740
They've had an accident.

122
00:04:32,740 --> 00:04:33,920
They've been arrested.

123
00:04:33,920 --> 00:04:35,840
They need money. Now.

124
00:04:35,840 --> 00:04:39,570
America's Federal Trade Commission
has warned about exactly this.

125
00:04:39,570 --> 00:04:42,120
It calls it the family emergency scam.

126
00:04:42,120 --> 00:04:46,520
And here in the UK, in a
2024 survey for Starling,

127
00:04:46,520 --> 00:04:50,290
more than a quarter of adults said
they'd been targeted by a voice-cloning

128
00:04:50,290 --> 00:04:51,940
scam in the past year.

129
00:04:51,940 --> 00:04:53,780
And it's not just voices.

130
00:04:53,780 --> 00:04:58,060
In 2024, a finance worker in Hong
Kong joined a video call with

131
00:04:58,060 --> 00:05:01,540
his company's chief financial
officer and a few colleagues.

132
00:05:01,540 --> 00:05:02,840
He recognised them.

133
00:05:02,840 --> 00:05:04,290
He did what they asked.

134
00:05:04,310 --> 00:05:07,580
And he sent about
twenty-five million dollars.

135
00:05:07,580 --> 00:05:12,790
Hong Kong police later said every single
person he saw on that call... was fake.

136
00:05:12,830 --> 00:05:14,290
All deepfakes.

137
00:05:14,400 --> 00:05:16,360
The company turned out to be Arup.

138
00:05:16,360 --> 00:05:18,150
A British engineering firm.

139
00:05:18,150 --> 00:05:19,610
I used to work in banking.

140
00:05:19,610 --> 00:05:22,300
And trust me, the scam call isn't new.

141
00:05:22,300 --> 00:05:23,220
What's new...

142
00:05:23,220 --> 00:05:26,140
is that it sounds exactly
like someone you love.

143
00:05:26,140 --> 00:05:27,760
Danger number five.

144
00:05:27,760 --> 00:05:29,190
The quietest one.

145
00:05:29,300 --> 00:05:30,940
Your own judgment.

146
00:05:30,940 --> 00:05:33,830
The more we use AI, the more we trust it.

147
00:05:33,830 --> 00:05:35,360
There's even a name for it.

148
00:05:35,360 --> 00:05:37,100
Automation bias.

149
00:05:37,100 --> 00:05:39,650
We trust the machine over our own gut.

150
00:05:39,650 --> 00:05:42,150
And AI always sounds confident.

151
00:05:42,150 --> 00:05:43,040
Polished.

152
00:05:43,040 --> 00:05:44,950
Even when it's completely wrong.

153
00:05:44,950 --> 00:05:48,730
Last year, England's High Court
dealt with two cases where fake

154
00:05:48,730 --> 00:05:51,740
legal citations ended
up in front of judges.

155
00:05:51,740 --> 00:05:55,570
In one, the research had been
done partly with AI tools.

156
00:05:55,570 --> 00:05:57,710
Forty-five cases cited.

157
00:05:57,710 --> 00:05:59,990
Eighteen of them... didn't exist.

158
00:05:59,990 --> 00:06:04,630
The judges warned of "serious implications
for the administration of justice".

159
00:06:04,720 --> 00:06:06,620
These were trained professionals.

160
00:06:06,620 --> 00:06:09,030
And the confident answer still won.

161
00:06:09,030 --> 00:06:11,590
And that's what makes this one the worst.

162
00:06:11,590 --> 00:06:14,250
If we stop checking, we overshare.

163
00:06:14,300 --> 00:06:16,450
We accept unfair answers.

164
00:06:16,450 --> 00:06:17,870
We believe the fake photo.

165
00:06:17,870 --> 00:06:19,730
And we pick up the phone...

166
00:06:19,730 --> 00:06:21,170
and trust the voice.

167
00:06:21,170 --> 00:06:21,890
So.

168
00:06:21,890 --> 00:06:23,060
What do you actually do?

169
00:06:23,060 --> 00:06:25,830
You don't have to throw
your phone in the sea.

170
00:06:25,830 --> 00:06:26,990
4 habits.

171
00:06:26,990 --> 00:06:27,650
One.

172
00:06:27,650 --> 00:06:29,030
The postcard rule.

173
00:06:29,030 --> 00:06:32,480
Nothing goes into a chatbot that
you wouldn't put on a postcard.

174
00:06:32,480 --> 00:06:34,760
And switch off training wherever you can.

175
00:06:34,760 --> 00:06:35,420
Two.

176
00:06:35,420 --> 00:06:36,460
Question it.

177
00:06:36,460 --> 00:06:39,760
If an answer matters, ask
the AI for its sources...

178
00:06:39,760 --> 00:06:41,530
then go and check them yourself.

179
00:06:41,540 --> 00:06:43,990
Because AI can make up sources too.

180
00:06:43,990 --> 00:06:44,790
3.

181
00:06:44,790 --> 00:06:46,580
And this is the trick I promised.

182
00:06:46,580 --> 00:06:48,950
Agree a safe phrase with your family.

183
00:06:48,950 --> 00:06:49,870
Tonight.

184
00:06:49,870 --> 00:06:52,650
A word or a phrase that only you know.

185
00:06:52,650 --> 00:06:54,210
Say it face to face.

186
00:06:54,210 --> 00:06:55,290
Never text it.

187
00:06:55,290 --> 00:06:56,540
Never post it.

188
00:06:56,540 --> 00:06:59,950
If "Mum" ever calls in a
panic asking for money...

189
00:06:59,950 --> 00:07:01,340
ask for the phrase.

190
00:07:01,340 --> 00:07:02,200
No phrase?

191
00:07:02,200 --> 00:07:03,010
Hang up.

192
00:07:03,010 --> 00:07:05,590
And call her back on the
number you already have.

193
00:07:05,590 --> 00:07:06,510
And 4.

194
00:07:06,510 --> 00:07:10,220
Use AI as a co-pilot, not an autopilot.

195
00:07:10,230 --> 00:07:13,170
Let it draft, brainstorm, explain.

196
00:07:13,170 --> 00:07:15,250
But the final call is yours.

197
00:07:15,250 --> 00:07:18,240
If you can't explain its
answer in your own words,

198
00:07:18,240 --> 00:07:19,560
you haven't learned it.

199
00:07:19,560 --> 00:07:21,150
You've just copied it.

200
00:07:21,150 --> 00:07:23,240
AI isn't the villain here.

201
00:07:23,240 --> 00:07:26,230
The stranger in your phone
can be brilliant company.

202
00:07:26,230 --> 00:07:28,190
Just don't hand him the keys.

203
00:07:28,190 --> 00:07:31,400
There's one word in this
video I never said out loud.

204
00:07:31,400 --> 00:07:32,330
Spotted it?

205
00:07:32,330 --> 00:07:33,140
Comment it.

206
00:07:33,140 --> 00:07:36,820
Subscribe for more AI,
explained like this.
